NAME
    pack - compact the volume, reclaiming dead sectors

SYNOPSIS
    pack
    pack -h

DESCRIPTION
    Files and directories are single contiguous extents, and new ones
    are only ever allocated at the volume's free pointer, so a sector
    that stops being used stays dead: a deleted or replaced file, the
    pipes' /PIPE0.TMP and /PIPE1.TMP, the old copy a >> append leaves
    when the file was not the last one written, a removed directory.
    pack slides every live file and directory down over those holes,
    in disk order, updates each directory entry to the new place, and
    lowers the free pointer in the boot block. It then prints:

        pack: 9 sectors reclaimed, free pointer 645 (was 654)
        pack: 64 files and 5 directories moved

    The current directory stays current (pack re-enters it by its
    path, since the directory itself may have moved), and the shell
    re-reads the free pointer when pack returns, so the next file is
    written at the new free pointer. Each directory's '.' and '..'
    are checked and rewritten where they are wrong. Deleted entries
    stay in their directories as tombstones: the OS reuses them for
    new entries, so no room is lost there.

    pack refuses to run with a < or > or >> or inside a pipe: those
    are files the shell holds open, and a file open for writing grows
    at the old free pointer. It refuses a volume whose extents
    overlap or reach past the free pointer, before writing anything
    (check such a card with tools/p8xfs.py fsck on the host).
    pack is /BIN/PACK.

LIMITS
    800 files and directories on the volume. Only sectors behind the
    first hole are copied, one sector at a time: a pack after deleting
    a file near the start of the disk moves nearly everything.

WARNINGS
    There is no journal. Do not reset or switch off while pack runs.
    What an interruption leaves behind:
      - The volume's structure stays sound: every entry points at a
        whole extent in the data area, no two extents overlap, the
        free pointer is above them all. So running pack again after
        an interrupted run is safe; it finishes the job and repairs
        any '.' or '..' the interrupted run had not fixed yet.
      - The one file or directory being copied when the power went
        can be lost. If its hole was at least as big as itself, its
        old copy is untouched and its entry still points there: no
        harm. If the hole was smaller, the copy had already written
        over the start of its own old place, and the entry still
        points there: that file is garbage, and for a directory the
        files under it are lost with it (it usually reads as empty,
        and p8xfs.py fsck does not notice). pack cannot tell
        afterwards and cannot repair it. After deleting a small file
        that is most moves: any extent bigger than the hole before it.
      - As with any write, a sector half-written when the power drops
        (a directory sector, or the boot block at the very end) can
        be damaged.
    Before packing a card that matters, copy it (dd on the host), and
    after an interrupted pack check it with p8xfs.py fsck and look at
    the files that were being moved.

EXAMPLES
    del /OLD.TXT
    pack
    dir

NOTES
    New in Y1/OS (2026-09-23). On the P8X, PACK was a shell built-in;
    here it is a program, working on raw sectors through the ROM's
    CFREAD/CFWRITE, so it costs the OS nothing.

SEE ALSO
    del, rmdir, fs
